            "guidance": "Install for uptime monitoring if you are comfortable with local state files and scheduled jobs. Before enabling Vercel or GA4, review the delegated skills too, use least-privilege credentials where possible, and keep includeMessages off unless you accept that application log text may contain sensitive data.",
            "findings": "[TP4] expected: Credential pass-through for Vercel and GA4 is real, but SKILL.md frontmatter and prose disclose the exact variables and openclaw.json keys, and the values are passed to the matching delegated skill rather than printed or written by Web Observer.\n[AE1] expected: The referenced delegated skills are runtime dependencies that may not be present in this package; this is not evidence of evasion, but users should review those optional skills before enabling Vercel or GA4 modules.\n[RA1] unexpected: The cited service file line is a comment telling users to edit the SKILL_DIR path in a copied systemd unit; it is not self-modifying code.\n[PE3] expected: The credential-related code reads configured OpenClaw skill entries and environment variables, not arbitrary credential stores; this is proportionate to scheduled delegated API polling.\n[RA2] expected: Persistence exists as state files, cron jobs, and an optional systemd timer, all disclosed and tied directly to monitoring and alert deduplication.\n[SDI-4] expected: The docs distinguish side-effect-free check from scheduled watch/vercel-watch/digest commands that update alert state; the inspected code matches that distinction.\n[SQP-2] expected: Vercel token setup is documented through OpenClaw configuration; additional file-permission and token-rotation guidance would be useful, but the behavior is not hidden.\n[LP3] expected: Network, filesystem, environment, and subprocess use are significant but narrowly described: uptime contacts configured URLs, state lives under OpenClaw state, and subprocesses are spawned without a shell.",
